thod, equipment ID, calibration record and signed observations for a real laboratory result.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 58]\nThe model shows streamlines, wavy transitional profiles and turbulent eddies; identify the tested geometry.\nIllustrate an ordered dye filament versus turbulent mixing; identify geometry, velocity, density, viscosity and temperature. The familiar pipe threshold is not a universal river classification threshold.\nThe pictured setup illustrates the relevant equipment or model. It is not authenticated footage from a completed measurement.\nA real result also depends on the specified method, equipment condition, calibration and identifiable source record.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 59]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  WORKING METHOD\nRAREMINDS\nFollow the process: input → action → observed result\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n59\n1\nSOURCE\nFlow regime depends on inertia versus viscous effects; pipe Reynolds rules do not transfer directly to natural rivers.\n2\nOPERATION\nLaminar layers have limited cross-stream mixing; turbulence produces eddies; rough open channels have free surfaces and different regime criteria\n3\nCHECK\nOpen-channel river classification requires hydraulic radius and often Froude number; Re threshold differs.\nVISIBLE RESULT / CAUTION: Open-channel river classification requires hydraulic radius and often Froude number; Re threshold differs.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 59]\nThe scientific sequence begins with this relationship: Laminar layers have limited cross-stream mixing.\nThe connected effect is described by Turbulence produces eddies.\nReynolds number compares inertial and viscous tendencies. Smooth-laminar flow, transitional behaviour and fully turbulent eddies arise under different flow conditions. The relevant length scale differs between round pipes, ducts and rivers.\nOpen-channel river classification requires hydraulic radius and often Froude number; Re threshold differs.\nThis separates a visible scientific response from the further testing needed for a real operational conclusion.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 60]\n","embedding":"[0.0031883924,0.01280413,0.01931633,-0.01897846,-0.010418688,-0.029377438,0.0016546388,-0.039692968,-0.022793407,-0.08280865,0.017274603,0.010137407,0.010299655,0.024544582,-0.028569978,0.0023118784,0.018059323,-0.029051919,0.0011089712,-0.010995756,0.03282587,-0.005021008,0.0010505888,0.0040135668,-0.047595866,0.025933443,0.024951603,-0.010580194,-0.018212259,0.13622107,0.0074258912,-0.037848312,0.0077743293,0.0068592667,0.030131819,0.019881172,0.0032486718,0.015273045,0.018140536,0.02120492,0.036800366,-0.030530814,0.0077634687,-0.0042386106,0.026243063,0.009883702,-0.00400409,0.028503733,-6.2430685e-05,0.013274838,-0.0057693506,0.0014632768,0.020270066,-0.02672944,0.008239154,-0.00071286,-0.0050687706,0.0045074346,0.032942098,-0.0069686593,-0.024368046,-0.012981562,0.0368517,0.0119343335,-0.027278302,0.0067036566,0.017073482,-0.027642893,0.01711465,-0.008867668,0.0069109127,0.002738632,-0.006652209,-0.0147929825,-0.0049934,-0.051710885,-0.07136869,0.005594399,-0.016070612,0.018072475,0.021927228,-0.0057653664,-0.006850786,0.029993238,-0.0340757,0.021158129,-0.014988958,-0.01276169,0.041447006,0.0602591,-0.019056357,-0.02663449,0.013041589,-0.030405762,-0.0105557125,-0.016375644,0.027380813,-0.03330421,-3.5314857e-05,0.0021775651,-0.031634692,0.0019435217,0.006935272,0.0096919285,0.018116275,-0.03616223,-0.020021621,0.014464491,0.012559765,-0.009415224,-0.024249043,-0.209968,0.028173108,-0.0047855913,-0