ebb252399a","analysis_id":"61b722f5-8838-4257-8470-376c62f24aa4","chunk_index":1,"content":"erage reporting\nWHAT YOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO DO\nExplain the mechanism, read the evidence, calculate a result, and state its limits.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 3]\nAn operational monitoring status must distinguish planned stations, accessible stations, actually collected samples and QC-accepted records.\nThis matters in environmental campaign execution and coverage reporting.\nThe important connection is between the mechanism, the evidence that represents it, and the conclusions that evidence can support.\nThe examples use illustrative values, so their scientific meaning is explained without presenting them as verified measurements.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 4]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 1  /  FOUNDATION\nRAREMINDS\nUnderstand the idea: monitoring objectives and station selection\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n4\nEXACT SCIENTIFIC MEANING\nAn operational monitoring status must distinguish planned stations, accessible stations, actually collected samples and QC-accepted records.\nWHY THE IDEA MATTERS\nEnvironmental campaign execution and coverage reporting\nWHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN\nMarking approval or access as completed collection\nFROM FIRST PRINCIPLES\nA monitoring project progresses through planned, access-ready, collected, laboratory received and QC accepted statuses. Each requires different documentary proof and may be tracked by different owners.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 4]\nAn operational monitoring status must distinguish planned stations, accessible stations, actually collected samples and QC-accepted records.\nA monitoring project progresses through planned, access-ready, collected, laboratory received and QC accepted statuses. Each requires different documentary proof and may be tracked by different owners.\nIn an engineering setting, this is relevant to environmental campaign execution and coverage reporting.\nA common confusion is marking approval or access as completed collection.\nA clear distinction between the underlying property and a particular observed result is essential before applying a formula.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 5]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 1  /  DEFINITIONS & COMPONENTS\nRAREMINDS\nEvery part, symbol and meaning\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n5\nPlanned\nintended site\nReady\naccess and team cleared\nCollected\nactual dated sample exists\nQC accepted\nmethod/chain review passed\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 5]\nThe term Planned refers to intended site.\nThe term Ready refers to access and team cleared.\nThe term Collected refers to actual dated sample exists.\nThe term QC accepted refers to method/chain review passed.\nThese terms describe different parts of the same scientific relationship. Treating them as interchangeable would obscure what the equipment measures or what the formula actually calculates.\n[Sources]\n","embedding":"[-0.010679469,0.03835257,-0.021532996,-0.02352789,-0.017990762,-0.0379711,-0.020525735,-0.058788095,-0.015853856,-0.06482078,-0.015942287,0.0193764,0.00928241,0.034964085,-0.0117178215,-0.014477606,-0.00472231,-0.019093595,0.019753518,-0.009087256,0.0016552581,-0.024171574,0.006631661,0.03516611,-0.033634137,0.034036834,0.01872254,-0.0055462793,-0.028991539,0.11636285,-0.007438488,-0.027386647,-0.017965354,0.010999827,0.010434206,0.012940134,-0.009271989,0.0038473322,0.025908638,0.0345607,-0.0031765853,-0.028338034,0.014272267,-0.0037411863,-0.02601713,0.005263196,0.013364219,0.014088652,-0.011390673,0.01733276,-0.0066603073,0.004140751,0.01730