366113,-0.042212863,-0.016701391,0.032297395,0.0015554302,0.016884984,0.009976487,-0.0057594883,0.021805288,-0.0124875745,0.0049950858,0.042244546,-0.0008339501,0.0128614,0.010102981,-0.024164379,0.008455347,0.044252373,0.004690123,-0.016170043,0.011620889,0.0036300155,0.0019937777,-0.0017116006,-0.02784666,-0.013932527,-0.035321034,-0.0063147848,0.011125966,0.0063909036,0.012149185,0.018292451,0.034024384,-0.024920395,-0.013475187,-0.015272015,0.002359538,-0.0037565408,-0.006941751,-0.015743226,0.008740681,-0.01876953,-0.0054615308,-0.0109810885,-0.012987698,0.0017786922,0.0019922464,-0.02478151,-0.014951122]","token_count":1061,"source_location":{"name":"Course 15 — Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":6082,"extracted_start":2901},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"261488d9-e2b9-4d2c-86bd-c1a96ae77e57","analysis_id":"61b722f5-8838-4257-8470-376c62f24aa4","chunk_index":14,"content":"ty or situation.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 32]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 2  /  ERROR ANALYSIS\nRAREMINDS\nFind the wrong reasoning and repair it\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n32\nINCORRECT OR MISLEADING CLAIM\nPresenting a ranked matrix score as calibrated probability.\nWHAT THE TAUGHT EVIDENCE REALLY SUPPORTS\nRisk matrix number orders attention; it is not a quantified illness probability.  A 12/25 risk score is not 48% probability of injury.\nWHY THE ERROR MATTERS\nCompetent site safety officer authorises controls/work\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 32]\nThe incorrect claim is presenting a ranked matrix score as calibrated probability.\nRisk matrix number orders attention; it is not a quantified illness probability. A 12/25 risk score is not 48% probability of injury.\nThe error matters because it ignores this boundary: competent site safety officer authorises controls/work.\nThe correction rests on the actual evidence and model, rather than on a rule remembered without its conditions.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 33]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 2  /  ACADEMIC GLOSSARY\nRAREMINDS\nRelated words and their precise meanings\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n33\nHazard\ntoxic or physical source\nPathway\nhow receptor contacts hazard\nLikelihood tier\nordinal\nSeverity tier\nordinal\nHazard\npotential source of harm\nExposure\nroute of contact\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 33]\nThe term Hazard means toxic or physical source.\nThe term Pathway means how receptor contacts hazard.\nThe term Likelihood tier means ordinal.\nThe term Severity tier means ordinal.\nThe term Hazard means potential source of harm.\nThe term Exposure means route of contact.\nThese definitions belong together because the model uses more than one quantity, structure or process. The words are precise scientific terms, not interchangeable labels.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 34]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 2  /  APPLY INDEPENDENTLY\nRAREMINDS\nNew case — answer before opening the next slide\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n34\nNEW SOURCE RECORD\nOrdinal likelihood=3 of 5; severity=4 of 5; after control likelihood=2\nYOUR SCIENTIFIC QUESTION\nCalculate initial and residual p