4868985,0.016571734,-0.0046709334,0.004285344,-0.03886887,0.00954625,0.039400924,-0.0104670515,0.016873727,0.01373807,-0.024756847,0.017953765,-0.006087498,0.011769677,0.04045282,-0.030319126,0.008507012,0.01506872,0.007103129,-0.0012481958,0.03759236,0.01193939,0.013680925,0.024702022,-0.0032631706,0.03409072,0.019964166,-0.038255706,-0.014969697,-0.056205448,-0.0030719351,-0.01609803,-0.0020184976,0.0062692193,-0.0093224915,0.03330044,-0.017537957,0.016435709,0.007356149,-0.0012618701,0.023970371,-0.01004663,0.023025578,0.0060974015,0.0051101,-0.02311178,0.006069754,-0.0017914559,-0.014299078,0.026667653,-0.01025589,0.0003698917]","token_count":1048,"source_location":{"name":"Course 04 — Fluid Mechanics and River Hydraulics (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":79945,"extracted_start":76801},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:29.161609+00:00"},{"id":"3672f65c-3617-4818-bf2a-14136376c5d5","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":2,"content":"efers to m/s.\nThe term Segment width/depth refers to geometry.\nThe term Discharge Q refers to m³/s.\nThese terms describe different parts of the same scientific relationship. Treating them as interchangeable would obscure what the equipment measures or what the formula actually calculates.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 6]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  MECHANISM\nRAREMINDS\nWhat physically or chemically changes?\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n6\n1\nVelocity varies with depth and bank friction\n2\nsum each subsection area × mean subsection velocity\nWHY THIS HAPPENS\nWater velocity in a river varies with depth and position; banks, bed friction and channel shape create gradients. Divide the section into bands and add each area–velocity product instead of assuming one point describes the whole channel.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 6]\nFirst, velocity varies with depth and bank friction.\nNext, sum each subsection area × mean subsection velocity.\nThe sequence is a scientific explanation: the visible outcome follows from the preceding conditions, rather than appearing as an unexplained result.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 7]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  APPARATUS & OBSERVATION\nRAREMINDS\nConnect the science to real equipment and evidence\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n7\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nWHAT THE LEARNER MUST OBSERVE\nShow a labelled bathymetric cross-section and three segment-specific Q contributions.\nHOW THE EVIDENCE IS PRODUCED\nA field cross-section needs tape width, sounding depth, station, timestamp and averaged velocity for each segment. A flow meter reading is not a total cross-sectional discharge until integrated.\nUse authentic method, equipment ID, calibration record and signed observations for a real laboratory result.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 7]\nThe model shows a labelled bathymetric cross-section and three segment-specific Q contributions.\nA field cross-section needs tape width, sounding depth, station, timestamp and averaged velocity for each segment. A flow meter reading is not a total cross-sectional discharge until integrated.\nThe pictured setup illustrates the relevant equipment or model. It is not authenticated footage from a completed measurement.\nA real result also depends on the specified method, equipment condition, calibration and identifiable source record.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 8]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  WORKING METHOD\nRAREMINDS\nFollow the process: input → action → observed result\n","embedding":"[-0.008670993,0.019509396,0.013933164,-0.028233673,-0.019402636,-0.048490204,0.0024554248,-0.054575637,-0.011963791,-0.08569231,0.017743876,0.0021489316,0.0057995375,0.008168409,-0.0046249544,0.01304257,0.01710192,-0.025078477,0.00528362,-0.029076518,0.04870318,-0.006665304,-0.014791025,0.014006025,-0.07302149,0.036383916,0.04685754,-0.037095677,-0.023635684,0.11857759,0.016535543,-0.029865734,-0.033972215,0.016468687,0.020662094,0.017616123,-0.004453376,0.009155078,-0.0059969644,0.02420639,0.020827712,-0.023815645,-0.0071135266,0.0026771782,0.043057587,0.007111053,-0.007260976,0.040659174,-0.015347873,-0.011590364,-0.01417446,0.0050772955,0.031432196,-0.01419287,-0.0146034,-0.004271779,0.006121766,0.016610838,0.03616671,0.012448906,-0.0037074815,0.0132871885,0.0329043,0.012996107,-0.012647144,-0.00254715,0.0064125704,-0.022222418,0.015421571,0.013831125,-0.005627206,0.010551668,-0.024679339,0.019291088,0.013679542,-0.050894823,-0.06581094,0.019625567,-0.017561033,0.0146194585,-0.0010720392,-0.012642885,-0.014469484,0.016107073,-0.017736232,0.0016812271,-0.039372195,-0.009907315,0.019858666,0.040542692,-0.0075582634,-0.026905987,0.014189773,-0.023566911,-0.031001193,0.003014826,0.04835771,-0.04999603,0.012055273,-0.024121407,-0.040859602,0.014498395,0.01050991,0.013446351,0.0018591471,-0.009415033,-0.018523056,0.011388704,0.0024571302,-0.015474431,-0.021278502,-0.2029438,0.026829826,0.02423672,-0.012017618,0.02899007,0.031948704,0.007024107,-0.025125476,0.0039021173,0.008728548,0.023786616,-0.022655845,-0.017714215,-0.0034139256,0.03418935,0.0150296725,-0.006955642,-0.011