 are flattened.","extracted_end":8845,"extracted_start":5671},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:29.161609+00:00"},{"id":"3c8c156d-2aa3-4349-bb65-6c2cca43f126","analysis_id":"61b722f5-8838-4257-8470-376c62f24aa4","chunk_index":7,"content":"eview passed.\nThe term Monitoring plan means required evidence.\nThe term Accepted record means QC-reviewed collection.\nThese definitions belong together because the model uses more than one quantity, structure or process. The words are precise scientific terms, not interchangeable labels.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 17]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 1  /  APPLY INDEPENDENTLY\nRAREMINDS\nNew case — answer before opening the next slide\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n17\nNEW SOURCE RECORD\nFive planned sampling sites, four access-ready, three collected, two QC accepted\nYOUR SCIENTIFIC QUESTION\nCalculate three distinct completion rates.\nWHAT TO SHOW IN YOUR ANSWER\nState the appropriate formula or mechanism. Show units and intermediate steps. Give one valid conclusion and one limit.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 17]\nThis different teaching case starts with five planned sampling sites, four access-ready, three collected, two QC accepted.\nThe scientific question is: Calculate three distinct completion rates.\nThe underlying principle is the same as in the taught example, but the data or conditions are different.\nA complete interpretation depends on the right relationship, consistent units, transparent calculation and a statement of what remains unknown. The result is explained on the following solution slide.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 18]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 1  /