etails/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 43]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 3  /  SOURCE RECORD\nRAREMINDS\nRead the values before calculating anything\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n43\nILLUSTRATIVE INPUTS — TAUGHT EXAMPLE\nWater flow Q=0.020 m³/s; total dynamic head H=5+10=15 m.\nWORK STEP 1\nHydraulic output=ρgQH=1000×9.81×0.02×15=2943 W.\nWORK STEP 2\nElectrical input=2943/(0.70 pump×0.90 motor)=4671 W≈4.67 kW.\nWORK STEP 3\nActual pump and system curves must intersect at the intended operating point.\nBefore a real report is accepted, sample ID, date, method, unit and quality status must be checked.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 43]\nThe given teaching inputs are Water flow Q=0.020 m³/s; total dynamic head H=5+10=15 m.\nThe first working relationship is Hydraulic output=ρgQH=1000×9.81×0.02×15=2943 W.\nThe next calculation or interpretation is electrical input=2943/(0.70 pump×0.90 motor)=4671 W≈4.67 kW.\nThe inputs and calculated steps are distinct. These figures are not presented as readings taken from a specific certified instrument.\nA field or laboratory report would additionally require an identifiable sample, method, date, units and quality status.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 44]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 3  /  FORMULA & VARIABLES\nRAREMINDS\nBuild the scientific relationship from its parts\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n44\nP_electric=ρgQH/(ηpump ηmotor)\nStatic head\nelevation difference\nFriction head\nflow-dependent loss\nPump curve\nhead versus Q\nMotor efficiency\nelectrical to shaft\nMODEL LIMIT / ASSUMPTION:\nRated pump curves, NPSH and electrical safety approval required\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 44]\nThe sci