slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":54168,"extracted_start":51084},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:29.161609+00:00"},{"id":"47053a4e-97d5-4a78-8604-5cf5205b8209","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":9,"content":"rms.\nIn an engineering setting, this is relevant to nozzles, sampling lines, flow meters.\nA common confusion is using Bernoulli without friction or elevation terms.\nA clear distinction between the underlying property and a particular observed result is essential before applying a formula.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 22]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 2  /  DEFINITIONS & COMPONENTS\nRAREMINDS\nEvery part, symbol and meaning\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n22\nPipe area\nπd²/4\nVolumetric flow\nQ=Av\nNozzle\nreduced diameter\nHead loss\nenergy dissipated by friction/turbulence\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 22]\nThe term Pipe area refers to πd²/4.\nThe term Volumetric flow refers to Q=Av.\nThe term Nozzle refers to reduced diameter.\nThe term Head loss refers to energy dissipated by friction/turbulence.\nThese terms describe different parts of the same scientific relationship. Treating them as interchangeable would obscure what the equipment measures or what the formula actually calculates.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 23]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 2  /  MECHANISM\nRAREMINDS\nWhat physically or chemically changes?\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n23\n1\nAt fixed flow, smaller cross-sectional area means faster average velocity\n2\ndownstream pressure depends also on losses and elevation\nWHY THIS HAPPENS\nConservation of mass means the same volume rate crosses both sections of an incompressible steady pipe. Halving diameter reduces area by a factor of four, which increases mean velocity by four when flow remains fixed. Pressure requires additional energy and loss terms.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 23]\nFirst, at fixed flow, smaller cross-sectional area means faster average velocity.\nNext, downstream pressure depends also on losses and elevation.\nThe sequence is a scientific explanation: the visible outcome follows from the preceding conditions, rather than appearing as an unexplained result.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 24]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 2  /  APPARATUS & OBSERVATION\nRAREMINDS\nConnect the science to real equipment and evidence\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n24\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nWHAT THE LEARNER MUST OBSERVE\nPipes rendered with measured diameters, arrows and a separate unknown pressure field.\nHOW THE EVIDENCE IS PRODUCED\n","embedding":"[-0.020952126,0.019463245,0.022909261,-0.023144169,-0.0051808693,-0.0536191,0.013712771,-0.041788526,-0.0005662704,-0.07781248,0.025831642,0.016031884,0.025116893,0.015420304,0.012452905,-0.009324652,0.009889465,-0.04709733,0.0011273832,-0.0074077183,0.02164651,-0.013276435,-0.010471541,0.020501524,-0.04307852,0.02201635,0.03195333,-0.03745597,-0.031566996,0.1139758,0.00018194452,-0.02833333,-0.007280512,-0.001113365,0.034151677,0.018612124,-0.011989118,-0.007363495,0.003911266,0.01852996,0.017890908,-0