973197,-0.009743709,-0.01464889,-0.0045623253,0.012150251,-0.01180005,0.01275091,-0.014466223,-0.002474955]","token_count":104,"source_location":{"name":"Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions","limitations":null,"extracted_end":310,"extracted_start":0},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:15.402426+00:00"},{"id":"4f336862-087b-4371-a91e-0f48df80a4c8","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":28,"content":"he numbers is necessary, but it is not the same as verification of an actual sample.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 64]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  SCIENTIFIC VISUAL\nRAREMINDS\nConnect the measured evidence to the mechanism\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n64\nINPUT / CAUSE\nLaminar layers have limited cross-stream mixing\n→\nPHYSICAL CHANGE\nturbulence produces eddies\n→\nEVIDENCE / RESULT\nrough open channels have free surfaces and different regime criteria\nReynolds number compares inertial and viscous tendencies. Smooth-laminar flow, transitional behaviour and fully turbulent eddies arise under different flow conditions. The relevant length scale differs between round pipes, ducts and rivers.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 64]\nReynolds number compares inertial and viscous tendencies. Smooth-laminar flow, transitional behaviour and fully turbulent eddies arise under different flow conditions. The relevant length scale differs between round pipes, ducts and rivers.\nThe diagram, chart or labelled schematic makes the scientific relationship visible, while the accompanying words identify the conditions being represented.\nThe illustration should not be mistaken for a calibrated field or laboratory observation.\nIts value is in connecting a mechanism to an observation or calculation; claims beyond that need additional data.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 65]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  INTERPRETATION\nRAREMINDS\nWhat is supported — and what is not?\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n65\nSUPPORTED SCIENTIFIC MEANING\nOpen-channel river classification requires hydraulic radius and often Froude number; Re threshold differs.  A smooth-looking river surface is not proof that the water column is laminar.\nCOMMON OVERCLAIM\nCalling every river laminar when water appears smooth\nPROFESSIONAL BOUNDARY\nUse fit-for-geometry turbulence model and measured conditions\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 65]\nOpen-channel river classification requires hydraulic radius and often Froude number; Re threshold differs. A smooth-looking river surface is not proof that the water column is laminar.\nA possible overclaim would be calling every river laminar when water appears smooth.\nThe professional limit is use fit-for-geometry turbulence model and measured conditions.\nA defensible conclusion says what the evidence establishes and does not silently extend it to an untested property or situation.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 66]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  ERROR ANALYSIS\nRAREMINDS\n","embedding":"[0.0020556126,0.012321898,0.011526262,-0.0104218405,-0.018653348,-0.039922442,-0.0016472583,-0.055278808,-0.004115279,-0.07305639,0.014637842,0.028502937,0.014426486,0.007306512,-0.021789154,0.0011658126,0.0039542164,-0.031088676,0.003166376,-0.0075420663,0.028402338,-0.0082931,0.008997796,0.02791636,-0.060217638,0