679,0.014174548,0.005012276,0.020616049,-0.027560392,0.00047145897,-0.010041929,-0.0088861445,0.019666806,-0.006037086,-0.0144380275,0.019609502,-0.026584124,-0.000569183,-0.038445055,-0.008696924,-0.019323306,0.019781444,-0.040333148,0.022702383,-0.011534792,-0.02153818,-0.0043689716,0.009502181,0.028901609,-0.018382432,0.009712057,-0.032274332,0.005928665,-0.022357944,0.015984006,0.027113056,0.0029050151,0.025846753,0.00672822,-0.04077607,0.044865333,0.021457322,-0.01127604,-0.010250873,0.0076327757,-0.0027563318,-0.0046596117,-0.0022615383,-0.038173255,0.014187389,-0.009030206,-0.004576037,0.0011192895,0.011174069,-0.018819915,0.012181204,-0.009252671,-0.001923905,0.020808456,-0.0029780506,0.01366378,0.023795927,-0.01612431,-0.004687644,0.042131793,-0.018153034,-0.0010489827,0.013920181,-0.0012629556,0.016655838,0.011806272,-0.02374867,-0.013674293,-0.023749184,0.000898907,-0.010157835,0.0055853515,-0.0050763967,-0.012944351,0.05627514,-0.021657825,-0.010880891,-0.00014640478,-0.01656736,0.014763311,0.00020049795,3.3245607e-05,0.0030028354,0.01756689,-0.019335687,0.009602713,-0.017561767,-0.0040752837,0.027669959,-0.026543813,-0.013437781]","token_count":1058,"source_location":{"name":"Course 04 — Fluid Mechanics and River Hydraulics (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":48488,"extracted_start":45316},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:29.161609+00:00"},{"id":"60c7e3b5-d432-4bfb-87ec-cbe851b72139","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":6,"content":"  ERROR ANALYSIS\nRAREMINDS\nFind the wrong reasoning and repair it\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n15\nINCORRECT OR MISLEADING CLAIM\nMultiplying entire section area by one maximum surface velocity.\nWHAT THE TAUGHT EVIDENCE REALLY SUPPORTS\nA single surface velocity is not an area-weighted mean discharge.  An area-weighted Q is a better model than maximum surface velocity multiplied by total area.\nWHY THE ERROR MATTERS\nCurrent-meter calibration and safe site procedures required\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 15]\nThe incorrect claim is multiplying entire section area by one maximum surface velocity.\nA single surface velocity is not an area-weighted mean discharge. An area-weighted Q is a better model than maximum surface velocity multiplied by total area.\nThe error matters because it ignores this boundary: current-meter calibration and safe site procedures required.\nThe correction rests on the actual evidence and model, rather than on a rule remembered without its conditions.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 16]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  ACADEMIC GLOSSARY\nRAREMINDS\nRelated words and their precise meanings\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n16\nCross-section area\nm²\nMean velocity\nm/s\nSegment width/depth\ngeometry\nDischarge Q\nm³/s\nDischarge\nvolume flow per second\nSubsection\nmeasured cross-sectional band\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 16]\nThe term Cross-section area means m².\nThe term Mean velocity means m/s.\nThe term Segment width/depth means geometry.\nThe term Discharge Q means m³/s.\nThe term Discharge means volume flow per second.\nThe term Subsection means measured cross-sectional band.\nThese definitions belong together because the model uses more than one quantity, structure or process. The words are precise scientific terms, not interchangeable labels.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-