/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 13]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  SCIENTIFIC VISUAL\nRAREMINDS\nConnect the measured evidence to the mechanism\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n13\nSegment\nWidth (m)\nDepth (m)\nMean v (m/s)\nQ (m³/s)\nLeft\n1.5\n0.5\n0.20\n0.15\nCentre\n2.0\n0.7\n0.30\n0.42\nRight\n1.5\n0.4\n0.25\n0.15\nTOTAL\n—\n—\n—\n0.72\nEach Q is measured cross-sectional area × representative mean speed for that band.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 13]\nWater velocity in a river varies with depth and position; banks, bed friction and channel shape create gradients. Divide the section into bands and add each area–velocity product instead of assuming one point describes the whole channel.\nThe diagram, chart or labelled schematic makes the scientific relationship visible, while the accompanying words identify the conditions being represented.\nThe illustration should not be mistaken for a calibrated field or laboratory observation.\nIts value is in connecting a mechanism to an observation or calculation; claims beyond that need additional data.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 14]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  INTERPRETATION\nRAREMINDS\nWhat is supported — and what is not?\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n14\nSUPPORTED SCIENTIFIC MEANING\nA single surface velocity is not an area-weighted mean discharge.  An area-weighted Q is a better model than maximum surface velocity multiplied by total area.\nCOMMON OVERCLAIM\nUsing only maximum stream velocity for entire cross-section\nPROFESSIONAL BOUNDARY\nCurrent-meter calibration and safe site procedures required\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 14]\nA single surface velocity is not an area-weighted mean discharge. An area-weighted Q is a better model than maximum surface velocity multiplied by total area.\nA possible overclaim would be using only maximum stream velocity for entire cross-section.\nThe professional limit is current-meter calibration and safe site procedures required.\nA defensible conclusion says what the evidence establishes and does not silently extend it to an untested property or situation.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 15]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  ERROR ANALYSIS\nRAREMINDS\nFind the wrong reasoning and repair it\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n15\nINCORRECT OR MISLEADING CLAIM\nMultiplying entire section area by one maximum surface velocity.\nWHAT THE TAUGHT EVIDENCE REALLY SUPPORTS\n","embedding":"[0.010376565,0.022564836,-0.0014182884,-0.013825573,-0.007124544,-0.042233165,0.011128893,-0.065578714,-0.019089373,-0.08234712,0.03007269,0.022484772,0.0153034115,0.013429834,-0.01887402,-0.010075628,-0.0007816325,-0.034293916,-0.017013611,-0.025660647,0.046894465,-0.02587674,-0.021990128,0.021569286,-0.053565327,0.012972795,0.048834097,-0.02030438,-0.02380667,0.14044423,-0.00031233783,-0.02122088,-0.018543756,0.0042243684,0.016558152,0.0107652135,0.0036944742,-0.011592936,-0.00053903944,0.018937362,0.0140732145,-0.03602297,-0.0072623244,-0.009847608,0.040319774,0.0058713374,-0.00874979,0.031567425,-0.0013937793,-0.011912344,-0.020859623,0.008334695,0.01415127,-0.022614384,0.01431195,0.019226523,-0.019900665,0.017146586,0.04663263,-0.01045215,0.0064261276,0.010476015,0.03670904,0.008076735,0.0012341642,-0.0041689924,0.0057735187,-0.03164779,0.021561876,0.009593651,-0.015374137,0.0046935175,-0.03209055,0.0053384546,-0.0036742168,-0.038419347,-0.0787338,0.010501515,-0.018935453