S Course 15 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 40]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 3  /  MECHANISM\nRAREMINDS\nWhat physically or chemically changes?\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n40\n1\nFeasibility requires comparing all candidate options against cost, time, evidence, technical suitability and authority—not only arithmetic headroom.\n2\nShow full six-row feasibility matrix, planned vs conditional budgets, vendor documentation and open gates\nWHY THIS HAPPENS\nFeasibility compares technical suitability, implementation constraints, schedule, evidence, cost and authority for every proposed option. A low-cost option can be excluded if the required method or permission is missing.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 40]\nFirst, feasibility requires comparing all candidate options against cost, time, evidence, technical suitability and authority—not only arithmetic headroom.\nNext, show full six-row feasibility matrix, planned vs conditional budgets, vendor documentation and open gates.\nThe sequence is a scientific explanation: the visible outcome follows from the preceding conditions, rather than appearing as an unexplained result.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 41]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 3  /  APPARATUS & OBSERVATION\nRAREMINDS\nConnect the science to real equipment and evidence\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n41\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nWHAT THE LEARNER MUST OBSERVE\nSix-option matrix with costs, eligibility evidence and signed decision fields.\nHOW THE EVIDENCE IS PRODUCED\nUse a complete six-option table, with criteria rows and fields for 