tural rivers.\nReynolds number compares inertial and viscous tendencies. Smooth-laminar flow, transitional behaviour and fully turbulent eddies arise under different flow conditions. The relevant length scale differs between round pipes, ducts and rivers.\nIn an engineering setting, this is relevant to mixing, pipe head loss and sediment transport.\nA common confusion is calling every river laminar when water appears smooth.\nA clear distinction between the underlying property and a particular observed result is essential before applying a formula.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 56]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  DEFINITIONS & COMPONENTS\nRAREMINDS\nEvery part, symbol and meaning\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n56\nρ\ndensity\nμ\ndynamic viscosity\nD or Dh\nhydraulic diameter\nRe\ndimensionless inertial/viscous ratio\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 56]\nThe term ρ refers to density.\nThe term μ refers to dynamic viscosity.\nThe term D or Dh refers to hydraulic diameter.\nThe term Re refers to dimensionless inertial/viscous ratio.\nThese terms describe different parts of the same scientific relationship. Treating them as interchangeable would obscure what the equipment measures or what the formula actually calculates.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 57]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  MECHANISM\nRAREMINDS\nWhat physically or chemically changes?\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n57\n1\nLaminar layers have limited cross-stream mixing\n2\nturbulence produces eddies\n3\nrough open channels have free surfaces and different regime criteria\nWHY THIS HAPPENS\nReynolds number compares inertial and viscous tendencies. Smooth-laminar flow, transitional behaviour and fully turbulent eddies arise under different flow conditions. The relevant length scale differs between round pipes, ducts and rivers.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 57]\nFirst, laminar layers have limited cross-stream mixing.\nNext, turbulence produces eddies.\nFinally, rough open channels have free surfaces and different regime criteria.\nThe sequence is a scientific explanation: the visible outcome follows from the preceding conditions, rather than appearing as an unexplained result.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 58]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  APPARATUS & OBSERVATION\nRAREMINDS\nConnect the science to real equipment and evidence\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n58\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\n","embedding":"[0.0020247162,-5.6890363e-05,0.03071988,-0.027463777,-0.0020650995,-0.028990952,0.017497884,-0.043759502,-0.0078054857,-0.08073303,0.022435319,0.0059371777,0.0053300564,0.011061804,-0.01713271,-0.0046949163,0.0131987715,-0.04798913,0.017358044,-0.0016620783,0.027222037,-0.008331603,0.009482987,-0.007287008,-0.04664741,0.007262182,0.013397305,-0.026561912,-0.011005221,0.12525448,-0.014331567,-0.044805646,0.0030635467,0.00089310005,0.04613791,0.01974639,0.009109287,-0.000627399,0.02769156,0.014572371,0.038969796,-0.035063997,0.023241775,0.002083036,0.024073325,0.0024651003,-0.014679537,0.03979086,-0.010660855,0.015717788,-0.0072320933,0.006867897,0.007542178,-0.020648269,0.0020878147,0.0032826154,-0.0086059,0.012011408,0.03435934,-0.018411532,-0.01988777,-0.008839755,0.0337