57866,0.008998424,-0.011061213,-0.04857318,0.0268303,0.02170015,-0.01253232,0.0010699246,-0.0029047723,0.014546623,0.0020414926,-0.011618592,-0.051589992,0.004859137,0.047008835,-0.0027084693,-0.005627177,0.007524663,-0.010822755,0.036446996,-0.010849262,-0.009351014,0.026841866,-0.0033656978,0.028800169,0.021146795,-0.020830737,-0.005906737,0.035314277,-0.0011974177,0.016619,0.016366592,0.0073184827,0.00046463023,0.010012241,-0.017497405,-0.0213069,-0.043480128,-0.020834662,0.0022497545,0.014628293,0.022305187,0.01147102,0.022828951,-0.009275466,-0.004515415,-0.015945507,0.008445313,-0.021923844,-0.015686082,-0.008104636,0.012957645,-0.023771845,-0.01053257,-0.0009557701,-0.0064711645,-0.00830576,-0.00817615,-0.027870426,-0.0004096215]","token_count":1066,"source_location":{"name":"Course 15 — Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":14585,"extracted_start":11389},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"8707e3c2-4e2d-4f17-8681-2abeac4a8535","analysis_id":"61b722f5-8838-4257-8470-376c62f24aa4","chunk_index":9,"content":"t; risk combines consequences with how likely a defined scenario is, often by an ordinal prioritisation matrix.\nWHY THE IDEA MATTERS\nWorkplace and environmental safety planning\nWHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN\nPresenting ordinal product as quantitative risk or universal safe/unsafe label\nFROM FIRST PRINCIPLES\nHazard means potential to cause harm; exposure is the contact route. Many organisational risk matrices use ordered likelihood and severity tiers multiplied for prioritisation, but those products are not calibrated probabilities.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 21]\nHazard is potential harm; exposure is contact; risk combines consequences with how likely a defined scenario is, often by an ordinal prioritisation matrix.\nHazard means potential to cause harm; exposure is the contact route. Many organisational risk matrices use ordered likelihood and severity tiers multiplied for prioritisation, but those products are not calibrated probabilities.\nIn an engineering setting, this is relevant to workplace and environmental safety planning.\nA common confusion is presenting ordinal product as quantitative risk or universal safe/unsafe label.\nA clear distinction between the underlying property and a particular observed result is essential before applying a formula.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 22]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 2  /  DEFINITIONS & COMPONENTS\nRAREMINDS\nEvery part, symbol and meaning\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n22\nHazard\ntoxic or physical source\nPathway\nhow receptor contacts hazard\nLikelihood tier\nordinal\nSeverity tier\nordinal\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 22]\nThe term Hazard refers to toxic or physical source.\nThe term Pathway refers to how receptor contacts hazard.\nThe term Likelihood tier refers to ordinal.\nThe term Severity tier refers to ordinal.\nThese terms describe different parts of the same scientific relationship. Treating them as interchangeable would obscure what the equipment measures or what the formula actually calculates.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 23]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 2  /  MECHANISM\nRAREMINDS\nWhat physically or chemically changes?\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n23\n1\nHazard moves through a pathway to receptor\n2\ncontrols interrupt source/pathway/exposure\n3\nassess residual scenario after controls\nWHY THIS HAPPENS\nHazard means potential to cause harm; exposure is the contact route. Many organisational risk matrices use ordered likelihood and severity tiers multiplied for prioritisation, but those products are not calibrated probabilities.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 23]\nFirst, hazard moves through a pathway to receptor.\nNext, controls interrupt source/pathway/exposure.\nFinally, assess residual scenario after controls.\n","embedding":"[-0.034491654,0.017552504,0.009577277,-0.007407719,-0.012089828,-0.02718329,-0.027182618,-0.048636727,0.013022035,-0.08833341,-0.0106521435,0.028961008,0.010499206,0.033598162,-0.0007240798,-0.0316191,0.012581898,-0.00739202,0.01990595,-0.02418232,0.002098178,-0.01310837,-0.018695198,0.03642091,-0.031185018,0.021603812,0.029035559,0.006762853,-0.008196727,0.10570237,-0.004463559,-0.020170579,0.00823663,0.008925919,0.026500389,0.008362139,-0.027547164,-0.0062852157,0.029166058,0.020680938,0.0020312183,-0.03575324,0.040422264,-0.020841936,-0.028532721,0.020917201,-0.019194162,0.022181638,-0.00088988815,0.027156252,-0.023063961,0.013290729,0.02937261,-0.023916766,-0.0099735055,-0.027044138,0.011396562,0.041234337,0.037022915,0.0504317,0.012463725,0.0017667174,0.016801465,-0.0024441348,0.009478115,-0.013163637,0.027082061,-0.01022754,-0.004612084,0.038324162,0.046102177,-0.0011891795,-0.029490879,-0.008490979,-0.024123682,-0.036929224,-0.04347259,0.009947084,-0.026457474,0.03043338,-0.006732462,-0.024229666,-0.035467096,0.009750222,-0.01680777,0.0098800855,-0.021101866,-0.028801853,0.023276709,0.03239581,-0.004100907,-0.021180172,0.031905353,-0.031779688,-0.043927606,-0.0044491473,0.03988684,0.0063710934,-0.028922876,-0.02839789,-0.040773373,-0.0028551647,0.017127331,0.011876635,-0.0036142394,-0.018040776,-0.03684358,0.03964466,0.019531757,-0.0027901516,-0.043944385,-0.18509129,0.007868734,-0.021130681,-0.0140418755,0.033018578,0.027429625,0.020205922,-0.022732439,0.02289547,-0.0050799735,0.006227511,-0.03