ed media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":28591,"extracted_start":25480},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"8c16d3d8-0455-4b82-bf55-b4cbe6760033","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":7,"content":"rent teaching case starts with three river segments: widths 1.5, 2.0 and 1.5 m; depths .5,.7,.4 m; average speeds .2,.3,.25 m/s.\nThe scientific question is: Find total cross-sectional discharge.\nThe underlying principle is the same as in the taught example, but the data or conditions are different.\nA complete interpretation depends on the right relationship, consistent units, transparent calculation and a statement of what remains unknown. The result is explained on the following solution slide.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 18]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  COMPLETE SOLUTION\nRAREMINDS\nHow the new case is solved and checked\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n18\n1\nFor each segment Q=width×depth×mean velocity\n2\nSum 0.15+0.42+0.15\nREASONED ANSWER\nQ = 0.72 m³/s. Each velocity applies only to its measured segment.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 18]\nThe new case is resolved by applying the earlier scientific relationship to the changed conditions.\nThe first calculation is for each segment Q=width×depth×mean velocity.\nThe next calculation is sum 0.15+0.42+0.15.\nThe reasoned result is Q = 0.72 m³/s. Each velocity applies only to its measured segment.\nThis solution shows why the method works in a second situation, not just how to repeat the number from the first example.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 19]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  INDUSTRY APPLICATION\nRAREMINDS\nWhere this matters in engineering work\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n19\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nACTUAL JOB APPLICATION\nWater abstraction, river load and flood assessment\nWHAT MAY BE REPORTED\nA single surface velocity is not an area-weighted mean discharge.  An area-weighted Q is a better model than maximum surface velocity multiplied by total area.\nWHO CAN APPROVE A REAL DECISION\nCurrent-meter calibration and safe site procedures required\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 19]\nIn professional practice, this science appears in water abstraction, river load and flood assessment.\nA single surface velocity is not an area-weighted mean discharge. An area-weighted Q is a better model than maximum surface velocity multiplied by total area.\nThe authority boundary is current-meter calibration and safe site procedures required.\nThe academic example develops scientific judgment; an actual operating decision depends on documented measurements, approved procedures and the responsible reviewer.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 20]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 2  /  TOPIC INTRODUCTION\nRAREMINDS\n","embedding":"[-0.019122953,0.01016491,0.01101722,-0.0070744585,-0.013473031,-0.05434068,-0.0040547214,-0.060623474,-0.0074252295,-0.07403792,0.009933076,0.016333561,0.01839338,0.029856632,0.0026371472,-0.025317455,0.0050839805,-0.02879586,0.018476045,-0.024171075,0.025720885,-0.023394164,-0.006419175,0.033449173,-0.073081695,0.028310224,0.011169931,-0.029523458,-0.027100982,0.09565731,-0.0018032804,-0.028660946,-0.012519039,0.012596536,0.02189484,0.036487576,-0.014372142,-0.0081