ED FIELD FOOTAGE\nACTUAL JOB APPLICATION\nNozzles, sampling lines, flow meters\nWHAT MAY BE REPORTED\nDoubling velocity is not always equivalent to a fixed pressure change; energy balance requires head loss.  The continuity equation does not independently predict downstream pressure or cavitation risk.\nWHO CAN APPROVE A REAL DECISION\nValidate pressure rating and cavitation risk before operation\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 36]\nIn professional practice, this science appears in nozzles, sampling lines, flow meters.\nDoubling velocity is not always equivalent to a fixed pressure change; energy balance requires head loss. The continuity equation does not independently predict downstream pressure or cavitation risk.\nThe authority boundary is validate pressure rating and cavitation risk before operation.\nThe academic example develops scientific judgment; an actual operating decision depends on documented measurements, approved procedures and the responsible reviewer.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 37]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 3  /  TOPIC INTRODUCTION\nRAREMINDS\nPressure, head and pumping\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n37\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nTHE QUESTION WE ARE ANSWERING\nPump sizing needs total dynamic head and combined efficiencies, not only hydraulic output power.\nWHERE THIS IS USED\nPumping stations and treatment plant energy budgets\nWHAT YOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO DO\nExplain the mechanism, read the evidence, calculate a result, and state its limits.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 37]\nPump sizing needs total dynamic head and combined efficiencies, not only hydraulic output power.\nThis matters in pumping stations and treatment plant energy budgets.\nThe important connection is between the mechanism, the evidence that represents it, and the conclusions that evidence can support.\nThe examples use illustrative values, so their scientific meaning is explained without presenting them as verified measurements.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 38]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 3  /  FOUNDATION\nRAREMINDS\nUnderstand the idea: pressure, head and pumping\n","embedding":"[-0.007398887,0.010848163,0.0069647604,-0.010201491,-0.0016564984,-0.03503001,0.0016284739,-0.051212236,-0.0015328497,-0.07611181,0.016119983,0.027027505,0.007724037,0.005068789,0.0054097385,-0.0052066175,0.018880848,-0.038645282,-0.0047286404,-0.020525306,0.0060343486,-0.018841784,-0.000698565,0.009723683,-0.05410256,0.020147545,0.017857764,-0.03575969,-0.027773812,0.10397685,-0.010101788,-0.022918828,-0.02351533,-0.014107523,0.02978519,0.023016924,-0.015382063,0.006536342,-0.0041177385,0.0317582,0.018254561,-0.011051271,-0.0031967638,0.0010936079,0.008233394,0.015726397,-0.0030282896,0.025357168,-0.008636553,0.0146204345,-0.012146722,-0.006871465,0.019373273,-0.0071513546,-0.010665353,0.000507782,-0.042286642,0.031897083,0.03388232,0.024768224,-0.0013216488,-0.009518212,0.030491835,-0.0016865932,-0.019403704,0.0010366923,0.021007795,-0.017590262,-0.007948322,0.03837067,0.016680932,-0.003740703,-0.03127369,-0.0028155232,0.0010223819,-0.039178036,-0.07043054,0.023875844,-0.035953425,0.036576502,-0.0022598465,-0.02245356,-0.024542736,-0.0052705104,-0.03253065,0.017212193,-0.022008363,-0.016742403,0.022671813,0.03596763,-0.009946644,-0.004222258,-0.007867426,-0.026674988,-0.020007035,0.0023621928,0.030169211,-0.018445058,-0.017347943,-0.017327927,-0.029079782,-0.014519771,0.016639922,0.013059349,-0.003106484,-0.03380106,-0.010083353,-0.0060433517,-0.009853803,0.018255781,-0.045093432,