74753,-0.02496793,-0.008359334,0.0039168545,-0.015553363,0.009828579,-0.03722467,-0.0035037373]","token_count":1063,"source_location":{"name":"Course 15 — Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":45635,"extracted_start":42446},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"aa79f49d-e06e-4226-914a-f5e32d06c339","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":22,"content":"ll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n51\nNEW SOURCE RECORD\nWater Q=0.010 m³/s; total head H=20 m; pump efficiency 0.65; motor efficiency 0.90\nYOUR SCIENTIFIC QUESTION\nCompute hydraulic and required electrical power.\nWHAT TO SHOW IN YOUR ANSWER\nState the appropriate formula or mechanism. Show units and intermediate steps. Give one valid conclusion and one limit.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 51]\nThis different teaching case starts with water Q=0.010 m³/s; total head H=20 m; pump efficiency 0.65; motor efficiency 0.90.\nThe scientific question is: Compute hydraulic and required electrical power.\nThe underlying principle is the same as in the taught example, but the data or conditions are different.\nA complete interpretation depends on the right relationship, consistent units, transparent calculation and a statement of what remains unknown. The result is explained on the following solution slide.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 52]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 3  /  COMPLETE SOLUTION\nRAREMINDS\nHow the new case is solved and checked\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n52\n1\nP_h=1000×9.81×.010×20=1962 W\n2\nP_e=P_h/(0.65×0.90)\nREASONED ANSWER\nP_h=1.962 kW; P_e≈3.354 kW. Check actual pump/system curve for selection.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 52]\nThe new case is resolved by applying the earlier scientific relationship to the changed conditions.\nThe first calculation is P_h=1000×9.81×.010×20=1962 W.\nThe next calculation is P_e=P_h/(0.65×0.90).\nThe reasoned result is P_h=1.962 kW; P_e≈3.354 kW. Check actual pump/system curve for selection.\nThis solution shows why the method works in a second situation, not just how to repeat the number from the first example.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 53]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 3  /  INDUSTRY APPLICATION\nRAREMINDS\nWhere this matters in engineering work\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n53\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nACTUAL JOB APPLICATION\nPumping stations and treatment plant energy budgets\nWHAT MAY BE REPORTED\nThe hydraulic power is 2.94kW, smaller than required electrical input.  The lowest-power numerical option is not necessarily an operable pump at the required head.\nWHO CAN APPROVE A REAL DECISION\nRated pump curves, NPSH and electrical safety approval required\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 53]\nIn professional practice, this science appears in pumping stations and treatment plant energy budgets.\nThe hydraulic power is 2.94kW, smaller than required electrical input. The lowest-power numerical option is not necessarily an operable pump at the required head.\n","embedding":"[-0.0042902264,0.021286096,0.005767341,-0.016031848,0.0056414786,-0.026930837,0.015248563,-0.038609546,0.0017249135,-0.075827844,0.0059828013,0.013238052,0.013565286,-0.003258444,0