0.0007279811,0.012122736,0.019052833,-0.016716002,-0.023344247,0.019598752,-0.009082668,5.252278e-05,-0.056949914,-0.016660813,0.0059824535,0.010238336,-0.034701087,0.010055779,0.004978832,-0.029965822,-0.00020079887,-0.00898718,0.038292736,-0.012896729,0.008026971,-0.033255316,-0.0004283537,0.022942098,0.026562031,0.031895734,-0.018856527,0.023038067,0.007924971,-0.054941714,0.029244183,0.02785817,0.0063503706,-0.008033238,0.0006586024,0.0029297778,-0.0027371645,-0.0012962297,-0.035159152,0.024496812,-0.0014471834,-0.0151142655,0.00601415,0.004246819,-0.011631005,0.012787663,0.0067924783,-0.0084774345,0.041042224,-0.014121045,0.028094875,0.018003622,-0.025542958,-0.015322192,0.03430983,0.009811183,0.013895599,-0.00890458,0.00081489736,0.02711568,0.015670754,-0.027421106,-0.022566581,-0.034152456,0.005946926,-0.025544997,0.018116249,0.00785865,0.0153217,0.040817846,-0.013066785,0.026566667,-0.015240209,-0.022761362,-0.00387605,-0.0012596583,-0.0027173155,0.013169779,-0.0059117493,-0.015777668,-0.0014243702,-0.004712783,-0.022405582,-0.0008445788,-0.01958243,0.0085035255]","token_count":1038,"source_location":{"name":"Course 04 — Fluid Mechanics and River Hydraulics (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":65528,"extracted_start":62416},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:29.161609+00:00"},{"id":"ac83bf82-ce8c-425a-b2a4-183ce2ac68c6","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":1,"content":"nism, read the evidence, calculate a result, and state its limits.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 3]\nDischarge is volume per time through a section; rivers need segment-specific depths and velocities, not just one velocity at one point.\nThis matters in water abstraction, river load and flood assessment.\nThe important connection is between the mechanism, the evidence that represents it, and the conclusions that evidence can support.\nThe examples use illustrative values, so their scientific meaning is explained without presenting them as verified measurements.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 4]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  FOUNDATION\nRAREMINDS\nUnderstand the idea: discharge and cross-sectional flow\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n4\nEXACT SCIENTIFIC MEANING\nDischarge is volume per time through a section; rivers need segment-specific depths and velocities, not just one velocity at one point.\nWHY THE IDEA MATTERS\nWater abstraction, river load and flood assessment\nWHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN\nUsing only maximum stream velocity for entire cross-section\nFROM FIRST PRINCIPLES\nWater velocity in a river varies with depth and position; banks, bed friction and channel shape create gradients. Divide the section into bands and add each area–velocity product instead of assuming one point describes the whole channel.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 4]\nDischarge is volume per time through a section; rivers need segment-specific depths and velocities, not just one velocity at one point.\nWater velocity in a river varies with depth and position; banks, bed friction and channel shape create gradients. Divide the section into bands and add each area–velocity product instead of assuming one point describes the whole channel.\nIn an engineering setting, this is relevant to water abstraction, river load and flood assessment.\nA common confusion is using only maximum stream velocity for entire cross-section.\nA clear distinction between the underlying property and a particular observed result is essential before applying a formula.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 5]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 1  /  DEFINITIONS & COMPONENTS\nRAREMINDS\nEvery part, symbol and meaning\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n5\nCross-section area\nm²\nMean velocity\nm/s\nSegment width/depth\ngeometry\nDischarge Q\nm³/s\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 5]\nThe term Cross-section area refers to m².\nThe term Mean velocity refers to m/s.\nThe term Segment width/depth refers to geometry.\nThe term Discharge Q refers to m³/s.\nThese terms describe different parts of the same scientific relationship. Treating them as interchangeable would obscure what the equipment measures or what the formula actually calculates.\n[Sources]\n","embedding":"[-0.011458962,0.021989582,0.012936947,-0.017224768,-0.021461861,-0.053274546,0.013564838,-0.048486687,-0.0062339003,-0.06585422,0.012615015,0.021673506,-0.0074277003,0.02783189,-0.021965593,-0.0045443927,0.0020714914,-0.011745443,-0.002714427,-0.027678663,0.03640586,-0.0033938577,-0.017113954,0.027483333,-0.08000495,0.017729303,0.035768464,-0.033902474,-0.028348643,0.10438117,0.010762466,-0.036244884,-0.012233846,0.02014365,0.021410935,0.027478157,0.0031273395,-0.010601491,0.008891383,0.017993724,0.022221996,-0.02005138,-0.0019629884,0.007992964,0.03148213,0.016696801,-0.007379567,0.04200302,-0.01842328,0.0021330677,-0.016921615,0.015219514,0.03503732,-0.018336346,-0.007545877,-0.0067827585,-0.00064007845,0.027789135,0.047295276,0.015236841,0.0026486632,0.00067110575,0.037705086,0.02414803,-0.014789658,-0.008669348,0.022835838,-0.024810435,0.028014433,0.011051242,0.0047264025,-0.008655647,-0.016651101,0.015238589,0.009056936,-0.041747004,-0.0617235,0.025346711,-0.029344946,0.01777431,-0.007257482,-0.021583192,-0.003998099,0.024834877,-0.04186582,-0.00032882052,-0.03352857,-0.008345657,0.03875413,0.054122366,-0.012532907,-0.032414954,0.008921845