options\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n37\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nTHE QUESTION WE ARE ANSWERING\nFeasibility requires comparing all candidate options against cost, time, evidence, technical suitability and authority—not only arithmetic headroom.\nWHERE THIS IS USED\nResponse planning and engineering investment decisions\nWHAT YOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO DO\nExplain the mechanism, read the evidence, calculate a result, and state its limits.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 37]\nFeasibility requires comparing all candidate options against cost, time, evidence, technical suitability and authority—not only arithmetic headroom.\nThis matters in response planning and engineering investment decisions.\nThe important connection is between the mechanism, the evidence that represents it, and the conclusions that evidence can support.\nThe examples use illustrative values, so their scientific meaning is explained without presenting them as verified measurements.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 38]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 3  /  FOUNDATION\nRAREMINDS\nUnderstand the idea: feasibility and treatment decision options\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n38\nEXACT SCIENTIFIC MEANING\nFeasibility requires comparing all candidate options against cost, time, evidence, technical suitability and authority—not only arithmetic headroom.\nWHY THE IDEA MATTERS\nResponse planning and engineering investment decisions\nWHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN\nTreating under-budget as authorised; missing OP-05 and OP-06 from matrix\nFROM FIRST PRINCIPLES\nFeasibility compares technical suitability, implementation constraints, schedule, evidence, cost and authority for every proposed option. A low-cost option can be excluded if the required method or permission is missing.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 38]\nFeasibility requires comparing all candidate options against cost, time, evidence, technical suitability and authority—not only arithmetic headroom.\nFeasibility compares technical suitability, implementation constraints, schedule, evidence, cost and authority for every proposed option. A low-cost option can be excluded if the required method or permission is missing.\nIn an engineering setting, this is relevant to response planning and engineering investment decisions.\nA common confusion is treating under-budget as authorised; missing OP-05 and OP-06 from matrix.\nA clear distinction between the underlying property and a particular observed result is essential before applying a formula.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\n","embedding":"[-0.009298342,0.027305689,0.00055235345,-0.00907014,-0.0043201824,-0.0025457114,-0.04281697,-0.042999446,0.0035729215,-0.09595626,-0.0066190395,0.0125718815,0.009415379,-0.012005364,-0.0020901496,-0.0023568475,-0.0011224846,-0.006926516,-0.008569445,-0.010967336,-0.0051079956,-0.017664265,-0.017299457,0.023917638,-0.032784607,0.020994538,0.009611589,-0.025648046,-0.030435763,0.116616055,-0.0028450869,-0.01750887,0.0073774885,0.027336847,0.012616485,0.0039611612,-0.014560778,-0.001966029,0.054617584,0.015574858,0.026137393,-0.016208416,0.03649154,0.008422058,-0.006339524,0.0044713924,-0.009789565,-0.0018748564,-0.010366663,0.02275072,-0.020599974,-0.02315294,0.012687649,-0.0083605,-0.0036125947,-0.009207798,-0.012031524,0.016313273,0.029658198,0.023869442,-0.00026531416,0.00058849965,0.049409356,0.012391767,-0.017164644,0.008726953,0.027027505,-0.02215843,0.002836007,0.031861622,0.033641405,0.0039871037,-0.017903233,-0.011329051,-0.031712912,-0.034894593,-0.08596709,0.04102954,-0.014157475,0.013811806,0.0025608353,-0.012224