2649,-0.009149615,0.0050686994,0.021026665,0.01449211,0.017713208,0.050778218,0.0011029994,0.0072127827,-0.008019527,-0.0014488196,-0.014513181,0.012025463,-0.0036660742,0.046297062,-0.0012020944,-0.003987792,-0.009488314,0.0068215504,-0.01360554,-0.00621567,-0.027758677,-0.023241563]","token_count":1066,"source_location":{"name":"Course 15 — Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":48531,"extracted_start":45335},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"b7c73b29-72ad-4aae-955d-ab853b4f3a86","analysis_id":"61b722f5-8838-4257-8470-376c62f24aa4","chunk_index":0,"content":"[Slide 1]\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nRAREMINDS  /  ACADEMIC REFERENCE COURSES\nCOURSE 15 OF 15\nEnvironmental Monitoring Risk and Engineering Decisions\nUnderstand the science. See the working model. Calculate. Explain the result.\nFull learner-facing edition  |  4 complete topics  |  No separate workbook\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 1]\nEnvironmental Monitoring Risk and Engineering Decisions connects four scientific topics: Monitoring objectives and station selection, Hazard, exposure and risk matrices, Feasibility and treatment decision options, and Financial status, verification and closure.\nThe first ideas provide a foundation for the later mechanisms, equations and engineering examples.\nEvery laboratory scene here is a teaching illustration; it is not an authenticated experiment or a measured field result.\nThe course builds understanding of what the science means and what evidence would be needed to rely on it professionally.\n\n[Slide 2]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 1  /  COURSE OVERVIEW\nRAREMINDS\nYour learning journey — four connected foundations\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n2\n1\nMonitoring objectives and station selection\nLearn the meaning, how it works, a real-style record and a new calculation.\n2\nHazard, exposure and risk matrices\nLearn the meaning, how it works, a real-style record and a new calculation.\n3\nFeasibility and treatment decision options\nLearn the meaning, how it works, a real-style record and a new calculation.\n4\nFinancial status, verification and closure\nLearn the meaning, how it works, a real-style record and a new calculation.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 2]\nThe course begins with Monitoring objectives and station selection and then develops Hazard, exposure and risk matrices.\nThe third topic, Feasibility and treatment decision options, adds another scientific relationship; Financial status, verification and closure brings the four-part sequence together.\nEach topic connects the definition with a mechanism, a worked example and a separate application case.\nIllustrative quantities help explain the calculation; they are not substitutes for a laboratory or site record.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 3]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 1  /  TOPIC INTRODUCTION\nRAREMINDS\nMonitoring objectives and station selection\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n3\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nTHE QUESTION WE ARE ANSWERING\nAn operational monitoring status must distinguish planned stations, accessible stations, actually collected samples and QC-accepted records.\nWHERE THIS IS USED\nEnvironmental campaign execution and coverage reporting\nWHAT YOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO DO\nExplain the mechanism, read the evidence, calculate a result, and state its limits.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 3]\nAn operational monitoring status must distinguish planned stations, accessible stations, actually collected samples and QC-accepted records.\n","embedding":"[-0.0034838878,0.031669524,-0.0075288448,-0.014818612,-0.018837525,-0.029186646,-0.013014092,-0.043250076,-0.0050593247,-0.08186143,0.006196394,0.01418923,0.008362123,0.038717546,-0.0077827275,-0.016994206,-0.005589739,-0.016663156,-0.0005554444,-0.030148143,0.0126702655,-0.031320892,-0.0034241108,0.038001906,-0.015256235,0.019231474,0.011086606,0.0011988616,-0.029406851,0.13399531,-0.009718504,-0.03098385,-0.0020433462,-0.0019656767,0.014538268,0.01648825,-0.014483533,0.012864348,0.018724846,0.039437767,-0.008749011,-0.02587771,-0.0046336404,-0.014370733,-0.0209903,0.016221097,0.0055856723,0.01646566,0.0046349936,0.02701763,-0.008395162,0.00044956675,0.025908537,-0.009465619,0.021440376,-0.003110565,-0.0028017997,0.019693874,0.02758136,0.01946057,-0.008799121,0.005364155,0.036597755,0.005893944,-0.0075549767,-0.0014244033,0.024377106,-0.027909655,-0.006874675,0.024393097,0.023402777,0.010508294,-0.01647876,-0.017374838,-0.015459058,-0.054290686,-0.06465074,0.024926959,-0.042446557,0.015339815,-0.012475811,-0.01958526,-0.020045731,0.0054726154,-0.026276832,0.008711004,-0.021015108,-0.0065455325,0.015202839,0.01606724,0.009031621,-0.026188368,0.023476392,-0.01822479,-0.047867138,-0.0044862367,0.026489928,0.0044624032,-0.023719275,-0.03432432,-0.038061213,0.003351953,-0.0020226026,0.00037004548,0.016535487,-0.034260828,-0.036099304,0.03944542,0.012779897,0.0062347096,-0.044541255,-0.21188995,-0.0038238692,-0.022329694,-0.0055080797,-0.014188232,0.04321132,0.01660679,-0.0058578225,0.024090255,0.0062354174,0.013962507,-0.024042273,0.011010862,-0.040085576,0.019049162,0.013255579,-0.019524248,-0.028895792,0.004353548,0.021081463,0.01026012,-0.0018563977,0.022810725,-0.0038591835,-0.037164994,0.025520382,0.029895028,0.018974306,-0.008018373,0.029817868,0.00080877467,0.0056361756,0.003999089,-0.021960858,-0.008608863,0.0010093744,-0.0060452786,-0.0036938188,-0.0057566664,-0.01