-0.0006642402,-0.048677485,-0.014493504,0.0255093,0.0047788094,0.012946554,0.01841402,-0.025901541,0.029598402,-0.009362847,-0.0027669452,0.04254917,-0.00632312,0.025328508,0.008034358,-0.016458128,-0.0027823057,0.034963623,0.0020446905,0.012008293,0.01537282,0.01357601,-0.011824226,0.002059412,-0.018410334,-0.007907648,-0.03152866,0.004980313,0.0025367206,0.006546483,0.013226619,0.01382207,0.037508722,-0.010346565,-0.011719302,-0.021894602,0.008152857,-0.008606434,-0.0095262155,-0.016084634,0.007253909,-0.0099200485,-0.007464621,-0.0069011766,-0.015043279,0.009509137,-0.013302934,-0.027329272,-0.008374404]","token_count":1021,"source_location":{"name":"Course 15 — Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":8843,"extracted_start":5782},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"c0fbec26-3563-4ca3-8654-bcc6203da77a","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":14,"content":"nts on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n33\nPipe area\nπd²/4\nVolumetric flow\nQ=Av\nNozzle\nreduced diameter\nHead loss\nenergy dissipated by friction/turbulence\nContinuity\nconservation of mass flow\nHead loss\nenergy dissipated by flow\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 33]\nThe term Pipe area means πd²/4.\nThe term Volumetric flow means Q=Av.\nThe term Nozzle means reduced diameter.\nThe term Head loss means energy dissipated by friction/turbulence.\nThe term Continuity means conservation of mass flow.\nThe term Head loss means energy dissipated by flow.\nThese definitions belong together because the model uses more than one quantity, structure or process. The words are precise scientific terms, not interchangeable labels.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 34]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 2  /  APPLY INDEPENDENTLY\nRAREMINDS\nNew case — answer before opening the next slide\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n34\nNEW SOURCE RECORD\nPipe diameter changes from 8 cm to 4 cm; upstream mean velocity 0.8 m/s\nYOUR SCIENTIFIC QUESTION\nFind downstream velocity assuming steady incompressible flow; does this establish pressure?\nWHAT TO SHOW IN YOUR ANSWER\nState the appropriate formula or mechanism. Show units and intermediate steps. Give one valid conclusion and one limit.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 34]\nThis different teaching case starts with pipe diameter changes from 8 cm to 4 cm; upstream mean velocity 0.8 m/s.\nThe scientific question is: Find downstream velocity assuming steady incompressible flow; does this establish pressure?\nThe underlying principle is the same as in the taught example, but the data or conditions are different.\nA complete interpretation depends on the right relationship, consistent units, transparent calculation and a statement of what remains unknown. The result is explained on the following solution slide.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 35]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 2  /  COMPLETE SOLUTION\nRAREMINDS\nHow the new case is solved and checked\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n35\n1\nA1/A2=(8/4)²=4\n2\nv2=0.8×4\nREASONED ANSWER\nv2=3.2 m/s. Pressure requires an energy balance and losses.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 35]\nThe new case is resolved by applying the earlier scientific relationship to the changed conditions.\nThe first calculation is A1/A2=(8/4)²=4.\nThe next calculation is v2=0.8×4.\nThe reasoned result is v2=3.2 m/s. Pressure requires an energy balance and losses.\nThis solution shows why the method works in a second situation, not just how to repeat the number from the first example.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\n","embedding":"[-0.0075059277,0.015833508,0.016992787,-0.007618325,-0.009205165,-0.046467457,0.011725892,-0.03638744,0.011610392,-0.06335358,0.018502034,0.031204216,0.022126349,0.035023745,-0.0020985953,-0.015551423,0.024723731,-0.044455692,-0.013589659,-0.008658981,0.01070861,-0.022786567,-0.015056911,0.040491544,-0.046176303,0.008844041,0.012615685,-0.0368633,-0.039480772,0.098172285,-0.0035914204,-0.0422117,-0.0192931,0.0061462237,0.012941956,0.028860942,-0.014784109,-0.0051208693,0.008310883,0.012987842,0.017594036,-0.0059708487,0.027494617,-0.00253346,0.022938773,0.012741951,0.002196127,0.022921931,-0.013966761,0.028284186,-0.0064130393,0.0016843743,0.015616166,-0.0056945784,0.002663795,-0.022909962,-0.0058779484,0.04225099,0.027396824,0.017790476,-0.0053284992,-0.0016310258,0.04095778,0.015665192,-0.011611913,-0.0009037759,0.02116271,-0.019683458,0.0047914144,0.025556292,0.010127414,0.008160343,-0.044406917,-0.016704038,-0.0013257575,-0.056073654,-0.063674875,0.011944324,-0.021772575,0.047332097,-0.0060746185,-0.022757871,-0.0074406625,-0.0044246274,-0.045370486,-0.021150699,-0.016601935,0.00019608937,0.03911127,0.054277487,-0.0005573586,-0.005999507,0.0047479463,-0.018955668,-0.004870847,-0.009130245,0.031343743,-0.028391995,-0.008965049,0.0046024863,-0.030271893,-0.032634985,0.018028192,-0.0010249985,0.0035404875,-0.026371699,-0.01655624,-0.015679276,0.004481921,0.020215932,-0.0279854,-0.18231644,0.027013928,-0.0008569055,-0.032337483,0.057202585,0.023557443,0.008036977,-0.0058037117,0.0025304744,0.02013035,-0.011694481,0.009779303,-0.013872355,-0.01754359,-0.