,"token_count":1042,"source_location":{"name":"Course 15 — Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":57107,"extracted_start":53983},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"d97c8cb6-aadc-4ff8-8fe2-df6ceef6e8c1","analysis_id":"61b722f5-8838-4257-8470-376c62f24aa4","chunk_index":24,"content":"NDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 4  /  TOPIC INTRODUCTION\nRAREMINDS\nFinancial status, verification and closure\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n54\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nTHE QUESTION WE ARE ANSWERING\nApproval, commitment, invoice and payment are distinct states; closure requires evidence of completed authorised verification.\nWHERE THIS IS USED\nProject controls, procurement audit and case closure\nWHAT YOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO DO\nExplain the mechanism, read the evidence, calculate a result, and state its limits.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 54]\nApproval, commitment, invoice and payment are distinct states; closure requires evidence of completed authorised verification.\nThis matters in project controls, procurement audit and case closure.\nThe important connection is between the mechanism, the evidence that represents it, and the conclusions that evidence can support.\nThe examples use illustrative values, so their scientific meaning is explained without presenting them as verified measurements.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 55]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 4  /  FOUNDATION\nRAREMINDS\nUnderstand the idea: financial status, verification and closure\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n55\nEXACT SCIENTIFIC MEANING\nApproval, commitment, invoice and payment are distinct states; closure requires evidence of completed authorised verification.\nWHY THE IDEA MATTERS\nProject controls, procurement audit and case closure\nWHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN\nEntering zero where transaction evidence is absent; treating conditional approval as executed purchase\nFROM FIRST PRINCIPLES\nFinancial control distinguishes planned estimates, conditional authority, purchase commitments, invoices and payments. Each status needs its own dated evidence; closure is a separate professional decision.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 55]\nApproval, commitment, invoice and payment are distinct states; closure requires evidence of completed authorised verification.\nFinancial control distinguishes planned estimates, conditional authority, purchase commitments, invoices and payments. Each status needs its own dated evidence; closure is a separate professional decision.\nIn an engineering setting, this is relevant to project controls, procurement audit and case closure.\nA common confusion is entering zero where transaction evidence is absent; treating conditional approval as executed purchase.\nA clear distinction between the underlying property and a particular observed result is essential before applying a formula.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 56]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 4  /  DEFINITIONS & COMPONENTS\nRAREMINDS\nEvery part, symbol and meaning\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n56\nPlanned\nforecast cost\n","embedding":"[0.010352109,0.019578328,0.006609397,-0.0047197947,-0.010938067,-0.012407649,-0.00782012,-0.03530425,-0.0083632395,-0.09166206,-0.0039809416,0.02399702,0.011608585,0.020907959,-0.0024018127,