0.027914125,-0.009157741,-0.022407828,-0.027574059,0.0003088883,0.003675069,0.0091817845,-0.033715602,-0.017654568]","token_count":1030,"source_location":{"name":"Course 15 — Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":65578,"extracted_start":62488},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"def75ab2-dc92-400a-bdd8-ab812c2c24ed","analysis_id":"61b722f5-8838-4257-8470-376c62f24aa4","chunk_index":11,"content":"ted effect is described by Controls interrupt source/pathway/exposure.\nHazard means potential to cause harm; exposure is the contact route. Many organisational risk matrices use ordered likelihood and severity tiers multiplied for prioritisation, but those products are not calibrated probabilities.\nRisk matrix number orders attention; it is not a quantified illness probability.\nThis separates a visible scientific response from the further testing needed for a real operational conclusion.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 26]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 2  /  SOURCE RECORD\nRAREMINDS\nRead the values before calculating anything\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n26\nILLUSTRATIVE INPUTS — TAUGHT EXAMPLE\nRisk prioritisation matrix defines likelihood tier4 and severity tier5.\nWORK STEP 1\nInitial ordinal score=4×5=20/25.\nWORK STEP 2\nIf controls justify likelihood tier2 while severity remains5, residual score=2×5=10/25.\nWORK STEP 3\nThese scores are relative rankings, not epidemiological probability.\nBefore a real report is accepted, sample ID, date, method, unit and quality status must be checked.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 26]\nThe given teaching inputs are Risk prioritisation matrix defines likelihood tier4 and severity tier5.\nThe first working relationship is Initial ordinal score=4×5=20/25.\nThe interpretation is that if controls justify likelihood tier2 while severity remains5, residual score=2×5=10/25.\nThe inputs and calculated steps are distinct. These figures are not presented as readings taken from a specific certified instrument.\nA field or laboratory report would additionally require an identifiable sample, method, date, units and quality status.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 27]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 2  /  FORMULA & VARIABLES\nRAREMINDS\nBuild the scientific relationship from its parts\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n27\nExample ordinal score=likelihood tier×severity tier, not probability\nHazard\ntoxic or physical source\nPathway\nhow receptor contacts hazard\nLikelihood tier\nordinal\nSeverity tier\nordinal\nMODEL LIMIT / ASSUMPTION:\nCompetent site safety officer authorises controls/work\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 27]\nThe scientific relationship is Example ordinal score=likelihood tier×severity tier, not probability.\nIts symbols carry specific physical or chemical meanings and units. Changing the meaning of a variable changes what the equation can legitimately express.\nHazard means toxic or physical source.\nPathway means how receptor contacts hazard.\nLikelihood tier means ordinal.\nThe model has an important boundary: competent site safety officer authorises controls/work.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 11, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n","embedding":"[-0.010816117,0.01231798,0.0128635345,-0.028003698,-0.010468385,-0.