Not full release\nHeadroom\nINR 22,000\nDoes not prove authorisation\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 47]\nFeasibility compares technical suitability, implementation constraints, schedule, evidence, cost and authority for every proposed option. A low-cost option can be excluded if the required method or permission is missing.\nThe diagram, chart or labelled schematic makes the scientific relationship visible, while the accompanying words identify the conditions being represented.\nThe illustration should not be mistaken for a calibrated field or laboratory observation.\nIts value is in connecting a mechanism to an observation or calculation; claims beyond that need additional data.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 15 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://www.epa.gov/risk\n\n[Slide 48]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 15  /  LESSON 3  /  INTERPRETATION\nRAREMINDS\nWhat is supported — and what is not?\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n48\nSUPPORTED SCIENTIFIC MEANING\nHeadroom does not mean excluded work or procurement is approved; cheap action may be technically unqualified.  Unused budget does not authorise procurement or technical release.\nCOMMON OVERCLAIM\nTreating under-budget as authorised; missing OP-05 and OP-06 from matrix\nPROFESSIONAL BOUNDARY\nProcurement, technical approvals and public claims remain separately gated\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 48]\nHeadroom does not mean excluded work or procurement is approved; cheap action may be technically unqualified. Unused budget does not authorise procurement or technical release.\nA possible overclaim would be treating under-budget as authorised; missing OP-05 and OP-06 from matrix.\nThe professional limit is procurement, technical approvals and public claims remain separately gated.\nA defensible conclusion says what the evidence establishes and does not silently extend it to an untested 