26-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"f828c39d-3605-4d60-a963-e6c1b7483493","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":26,"content":"ientific response from the further testing needed for a real operational conclusion.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 60]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  SOURCE RECORD\nRAREMINDS\nRead the values before calculating anything\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n60\nILLUSTRATIVE INPUTS — TAUGHT EXAMPLE\nAssume circular pipe D=0.010m, mean v=1.0m/s, water ρ=1000kg/m³.\nWORK STEP 1\nAt test temperature assume dynamic viscosity μ=0.001Pa·s.\nWORK STEP 2\nRe=ρvD/μ=1000×1×0.010/0.001=10,000.\nWORK STEP 3\nThis classifies the illustrative pipe flow, not a natural river by the same threshold.\nBefore a real report is accepted, sample ID, date, method, unit and quality status must be checked.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 60]\nThe given teaching inputs are Assume circular pipe D=0.010m, mean v=1.0m/s, water ρ=1000kg/m³.\nThe first working relationship is At test temperature assume dynamic viscosity μ=0.001Pa·s.\nThe next calculation or interpretation is re=ρvD/μ=1000×1×0.010/0.001=10,000.\nThe inputs and calculated steps are distinct. These figures are not presented as readings taken from a specific certified instrument.\nA field or laboratory report would additionally require an identifiable sample, method, date, units and quality status.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 61]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  FORMULA & VARIABLES\nRAREMINDS\nBuild the scientific relationship from its parts\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n61\nRe=ρvD/μ for circular pipe; use hydraulic diameter for other enclosed ducts\nρ\ndensity\nμ\ndynamic viscosity\nD or Dh\nhydraulic diameter\nRe\ndimensionless inertial/viscous ratio\nMODEL LIMIT / ASSUMPTION:\nUse fit-for-geometry turbulence model and measured conditions\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 61]\nThe scientific relationship is Re=ρvD/μ for circular pipe; use hydraulic diameter for other enclosed ducts.\nIts symbols carry specific physical or chemical meanings and units. Changing the meaning of a variable changes what the equation can legitimately express.\nρ means density.\nμ means dynamic viscosity.\nD or Dh means hydraulic diameter.\nThe model has an important boundary: use fit-for-geometry turbulence model and measured conditions.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 62]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  WORKED EXAMPLE\nRAREMINDS\nWatch every calculation and the meaning of its units\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n62\n1\nAssume circular pipe D=0.010m, mean v=1.0m/s, water ρ=1000kg/m³.\n2\nAt test temperature assume dynamic viscosity μ=0.001Pa·s.\n3\n","embedding":"[-0.00423216,0.004333906,0.012946592,-0.020952603,-0.009541787,-0.03157222,0.01392764,-0.04067359,0.0006164419,-0.072455704,0.009979935,0.018883644,0.0008034705,0.0029171826,-0.013492811,-0.004113794,0.02176621,-0.043448165,0.002544412,0.0069459984,0.013426135,-0.006876203,0.009114435,0.028008755,-0.050354872,0.019942485,0.017902104,-0.022718616,-0.025078923,0.10799615,0.005787011,-0.033950664,-0.015264244,0.0050821723,0.032720666,0.01886826,-0.00032488714,0.001964699,0.016152686,0.021450728,0.038064662,-0.024840396,0.01833134,0.0009478156,0.024625