.001953944,-0.039609384,0.010315583,0.020421442,-0.014267091,-0.0040828334,-0.006789989,0.020212635,-0.01355389,-0.026223643,-0.047257066,-0.0052000037,0.022672445,-0.013604676,0.021872615,0.008980217,-0.021686636,0.022232583,0.01381503,-0.0022844737,0.046717808,-0.0059696585,0.019104645,0.01343467,-0.028676849,0.021848869,0.0075244578,0.0043627806,0.008248359,0.0005974681,0.01853063,0.015634352,-0.01877278,-0.025834663,-0.004604753,-0.03843229,-0.011131576,0.008416926,0.02025692,0.029354764,0.019379348,0.044204555,0.0054315496,-0.021820648,-0.024918636,0.018137876,-0.012341692,0.00078420684,-0.012438285,0.021538239,-0.017200263,-0.01529697,-0.016262978,0.0026843797,0.0013652386,-0.012728526,-0.037696704,0.008395211]","token_count":1065,"source_location":{"name":"Course 15 — Environmental Monitoring, Risk and Engineering Decisions (with speaker notes).pptx","limitations":"Extracted slide XML text, table cell text and speaker notes. Images, diagrams, charts and embedded media are not visually interpreted; layouts are flattened.","extracted_end":54283,"extracted_start":51090},"created_at":"2026-11-18T08:06:14.435833+00:00"},{"id":"ff487429-1901-4713-9932-66fd340ae784","analysis_id":"47ea0217-a6c9-4726-984b-c2e788d4a5b7","chunk_index":23,"content":"red\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 53]\nIn professional practice, this science appears in pumping stations and treatment plant energy budgets.\nThe hydraulic power is 2.94kW, smaller than required electrical input. The lowest-power numerical option is not necessarily an operable pump at the required head.\nThe authority boundary is rated pump curves, NPSH and electrical safety approval required.\nThe academic example develops scientific judgment; an actual operating decision depends on documented measurements, approved procedures and the responsible reviewer.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 54]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  TOPIC INTRODUCTION\nRAREMINDS\nLaminar and turbulent flow\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n54\nRECONSTRUCTED TEACHING SCENE  •  NOT AUTHENTICATED FIELD FOOTAGE\nTHE QUESTION WE ARE ANSWERING\nFlow regime depends on inertia versus viscous effects; pipe Reynolds rules do not transfer directly to natural rivers.\nWHERE THIS IS USED\nMixing, pipe head loss and sediment transport\nWHAT YOU SHOULD BE ABLE TO DO\nExplain the mechanism, read the evidence, calculate a result, and state its limits.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 54]\nFlow regime depends on inertia versus viscous effects; pipe Reynolds rules do not transfer directly to natural rivers.\nThis matters in mixing, pipe head loss and sediment transport.\nThe important connection is between the mechanism, the evidence that represents it, and the conclusions that evidence can support.\nThe examples use illustrative values, so their scientific meaning is explained without presenting them as verified measurements.\n[Sources]\nOriginal RAREMINDS Course 4 source slide 27, 28, 29, 30, 31, 32, 33, 34; teaching values are illustrative, not certified samples.\nhttps://openstax.org/details/books/university-physics-volume-1\n\n[Slide 55]\nRAREMINDS  /  COURSE 04  /  LESSON 4  /  FOUNDATION\nRAREMINDS\nUnderstand the idea: laminar and turbulent flow\nAll measurements on these pages are teaching examples unless an authenticated source is cited.\n55\nEXACT SCIENTIFIC MEANING\nFlow regime depends on inertia versus viscous effects; pipe Reynolds rules do not transfer directly to natural rivers.\nWHY THE IDEA MATTERS\nMixing, pipe head loss and sediment transport\nWHAT IT DOES NOT MEAN\nCalling every river laminar when water appears smooth\nFROM FIRST PRINCIPLES\nReynolds number compares inertial and viscous tendencies. Smooth-laminar flow, transitional behaviour and fully turbulent eddies arise under different flow conditions. The relevant length scale differs between round pipes, ducts and rivers.\n\n[Speaker notes for slide 55]\nFlow regime depends on inertia versus viscous effects; pipe Reynolds rules do not transfer directly to natural rivers.\nReynolds number compares inertial and viscous tendencies. Smooth-laminar flow, transitional behaviour and fully turbulent eddies arise under different flow conditions. The relevant length scale differs between round pipes, ducts and rivers.\n","embedding":"[0.0080436515,-0.005770732,0.02995745,-0.01622762,-0.0013820432,-0.032862242,0.003631768,-0.04039992,-0.0022515398,-0.08039517,0.010474222,0.0119262785,-0.001486726,-0.00038406168,-0.006125252,0.0039084177,0.0014565155,-0.036133736,-0.002140503,-0.00020340394,0.012368827,0.00017778129,0.005778525,-0.02585449,-0.052602034,0.013328951,0.029032126,-0.018516796,-0.016068885,0.104956895,0.0011310001,-0.022836128,-0.007011153,-0.003528934,0.020072382,0.0118650105,0.0075327475,-0.004321536,0.027192073,0.032752793,0.034646347,-0.03110283,0.014932581,0.00044248375,0.016927332,0.017275747,0.0011507137,0.028901266,-0.009498451,0.028397474,-0.0053100144,-0.02174749,-0.01096545,-0.031059265,0.009971075,0.003462701,-0.02458587,0.010679951,0.04103484,-0.015108837,-0.018266816,-0.023317154,0.04649915,-0.008081289,-0.020583343,0.008327896,0.022643896,-0.014203621,-0.0026509122,0.020441422,0.010227642,-0.005150276,-0.013206537,0.001402247,-0.00091926614,-0.05692951,-0.06156841,0.005206403,-0.025387917,0.015591642,-0.0017604404,-0.008447826,-0.007894934,0.01578279,-0.030827291,0.010625165,-0.016369265,-0.018072704,0.04556602,0.044948265,-0.007998709,-0.0110177975,0.0043421634,-0.028422846,-0.00095021457,0.008877138,0.025471816,-0.030984297,-0.02434023,-0.0093706595,-0.031531442,-0.007513923,-0.0018667603,-0.00072017324,-0.014309002,-0.03296801,-0.010600056,-0.016171118,-0.0059913835,0.0021544285,-0.023587922,-0.21307616,0.02040304,-0.00031451703,-0.013303392,0.026653